# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Class 5 Ch 4 We the Travellers - II: long-form tool descriptions (master prompt format), one per tool.
DESCRIPTIONS and OBJECTIVES are keyed by the tool media id."""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Balance the Sums\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Exploration (the free first try, right after the story video)\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, the opening activity \"Making Sums Equal\" (two groups of number cards, interchange pairs to make the sums equal in the fewest moves), numbers 1, 2, 7, 9 and 3, 4, 5, 9 (developer only). Practice values: 4, 6, 10 with 5, 9, 12, and 4, 9, 13, 17 with 6, 10, 15, 20.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student sees that swapping one card between two groups raises one sum and lowers the other by the same amount, so the gap between the sums changes.\n"
        "- Out of scope: the rule about half the gap (the video after this tool names it), groups of more than 4 cards, gaps that can never be closed.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A brass balance scale on a wooden fold-down tray table in the back seat of a car, with a blurred road and trees in the window (photo-style). The left pan holds four pink paper slips 1, 2, 7, 9 (Group 1); the right pan holds four yellow slips 3, 4, 5, 9 (Group 2). The beam tilts with the right pan lower. Under the pans: \"Sum 19\" and \"Sum 21\"; between them a tag \"Gap 2\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Nazrana's two groups of cards. Which group adds up to more? Tap it.\"\n"
        "- The child taps a pan. The right pan glows green and the strip says \"Group 2 has the bigger sum: 21 is 2 more than 19.\" Then \"Next\" opens Activity 1.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Balance scale: brass beam spanning most of the stage width, a centre pointer, two round pans. The beam tilts towards the bigger sum (about 5 degrees for a gap of 1 to 3, about 10 degrees for a gap of 4 or more) and is level, with a soft click, when the sums are equal.\n"
        "- Slips: torn-paper photo cut-outs, each a thumb-width across, with the number in large plain digits; Group 1 pink, Group 2 yellow; 3 or 4 slips in a row in each pan.\n"
        "- Under each pan a large sum label (\"Sum 20\"). Between them a gap tag (\"Gap 6\") that turns green and reads \"Gap 0\" when the sums are equal. A small \"Moves: 0\" counter at the lower edge. A dim \"Fewest moves\" star badge top right (Activity 3 and the star challenge only). Goal chip top left of the stage, for example \"Swap 5 and 6\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a slip in one pan: it lifts and glows orange. Tap a slip in the other pan: both glow and the main button reads \"Swap\". \"Swap\" (or dragging one glowing slip onto the other) makes the two slips glide across, the beam rocks to its new tilt, both sums count to their new numbers, the gap tag updates and Moves goes up by 1.\n"
        "- A swap is always one slip for one slip. A second tap in the same pan only moves the glow. Two slips with the same number (the two 9s) can be swapped; nothing changes and Moves stays the same.\n"
        "- No Undo: \"Reset\" puts this puzzle's slips back and sets Moves to 0. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\", and the main button (\"Swap\", then \"Next\").\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Swap the 4 and the 5 and watch the sums.\" Goal chip: \"Swap 4 and 5\". Setup: Group 1 is 4, 6, 10 (Sum 20); Group 2 is 5, 9, 12 (Sum 26); Gap 6; the 4 and the 5 pulse. Guess: before swapping, the child taps a chip for Group 1's sum: \"goes up\", \"goes down\" or \"stays the same\". Result: \"goes up\" turns green with a bounce; Group 1 becomes 5 + 6 + 10 = 21 and Group 2 becomes 4 + 9 + 12 = 25; the gap goes from 6 to 4. Why line: \"5 is 1 more than 4, so Group 1 went up by 1 and Group 2 went down by 1.\" Help: guided, \"+1\" floats up from the left sum and \"−1\" floats down from the right sum.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Make both sums equal with one swap.\" Goal chip: \"Equal in 1 move\". Setup: the same slips reset, Sum 20 and Sum 26, Gap 6. Guess: before touching a slip, the child taps how far each sum must move to meet the other: \"1\", \"3\" or \"6\" (right: 3). Then the child swaps 6 and 9. Result: Group 1 becomes 4 + 9 + 10 = 23 and Group 2 becomes 5 + 6 + 12 = 23; the beam levels. Why line: \"9 is 3 more than 6, so 20 + 3 = 23 and 26 − 3 = 23: both sums met in the middle.\" Help: guided, a small arrow runs from each sum towards the middle.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"One swap is not enough here. Make both sums equal.\" Goal chip: \"Equal sums\". Setup: Group 1 is 4, 9, 13, 17 (Sum 43); Group 2 is 6, 10, 15, 20 (Sum 51); Gap 8; the badge is shown. Guess: before starting, the child taps \"1 swap\" or \"2 swaps\". Result: no two slips are 4 apart, so 2 swaps are needed, for example 4 with 6 and 13 with 15; both sums become 47 and the badge lights. Why line: \"Each swap moved 2: 43 + 2 + 2 = 47 and 51 − 2 − 2 = 47.\" Any two swaps that end with equal sums are accepted (for example 9 with 10 and 17 with 20, which move 1 and 3); the strip says \"This way works too.\" Help: independent, no arrows.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- A swap makes and loses nothing: what one sum gains the other loses, and that amount is how far apart the two slips are (4 and 5 are 1 apart, 6 and 9 are 3 apart), so the gap changes by twice that amount. Equal sums mean both sums moved to the middle of where they started (20 and 26 meet at 23). One swap can be too small or too big; two small moves can add up (2 and 2, or 1 and 3).\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"A swap moves the same amount from one sum to the other.\" \"To make two sums equal, both sums must move to the middle.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the beam levels with a soft click, the gap tag turns green and reads \"Gap 0\", the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, and the strip shows the why line.\n"
        "- Mistake: taps the smaller pan on the opening screen -> red strip, small shake: \"Add each group: 19 and 21. Which number is bigger?\" Both sum labels pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong guess in Activity 1 -> \"Group 1 gets the 5 and gives away the 4. Is 5 more or less than 4?\" (the 4 and 5 pulse). Wrong guess in Activity 2 -> \"The two sums must meet in the middle. How far is 20 from the middle?\" (the gap tag pulses).\n"
        "- Mistake: swaps 4 and 5 in Activity 2 -> \"The gap is smaller, but not zero. Each sum must move 3, and this swap moved each sum only 1.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a swap that makes the gap bigger (4 with 20 in Activity 3, 2 with 5 in the star challenge) -> \"The gap got bigger: it was 8 and is now 24. Try two slips that are closer in value.\" (the real old and new gap; the swapped slips pulse). A swap so big that the beam tips the other way (9 with 15 in Activity 3) -> \"The beam tipped the other way: Group 1 is now the bigger sum. Try slips that are closer in value.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: equal sums in more moves than the fewest -> \"Both sums are 20 in 3 moves. Can you do it in fewer? Press Reset to try again.\" The badge stays dim.\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Which group has the bigger sum? Look at the gap tag.\" (2) visual clue: an arrow from each sum to the middle with its distance written on it (Activity 3: the gap tag glows). (3) almost the answer: the two slips that work pulse (the 6 and the 9 in Activity 2) and only \"Swap\" is left to press; in Activity 3 the 4 and the 6 pulse with the line \"Now find another pair that moves the same.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: swapping the two 9s: \"Both slips are 9. Swapping them changes nothing.\" In the star challenge only 2 with 3 works in 1 move. The tool never offers a gap that cannot be closed.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge. Goal line (the opener's own question): \"Interchange pairs of numbers between the two groups to make their sums equal. Try to do this using the least number of moves.\" Goal chip: \"Fewest moves\". Setup: Group 1 is 1, 2, 7, 9 (Sum 19); Group 2 is 3, 4, 5, 9 (Sum 21). Guess: the child taps \"1 move\" or \"2 moves\" first. Success: both sums are 20 after exactly 1 move, 2 with 3: 1 + 3 + 7 + 9 = 20 and 2 + 4 + 5 + 9 = 20; the \"Fewest moves\" badge lights with a bounce. Why line: \"3 is 1 more than 2, so 19 + 1 = 20 and 21 − 1 = 20.\" Equal sums after more moves are accepted but the badge stays dim. Help: independent, hints available.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"A swap moves the same amount from one group to the other.\" \"To make two sums equal, both sums must move to the middle.\" Think and talk: \"Two groups have the sums 30 and 40: how far must each sum move to meet in the middle?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: car back-seat window with a blurred road (photo); wooden fold-down tray table (photo); brass balance scale cut out in three parts (beam with pointer, two pans) so the beam can tilt; pink and yellow torn-paper slips (flat photo-style cut-outs); \"Fewest moves\" star badge. Digits are drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M1.S4.T4.C14.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Fuel Tank Adder\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S4.T4.C14.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, Fuel Arithmetic (the lorry with 28 litres + 75 litres, and 49 + 89, on the place value chart), developer only. Practice values: 47 + 36, 213 + 124, 64 + 95, 285 + 167, 506 + 195.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student adds two numbers one place at a time, starting with the ones, and regroups 10 ones as 1 ten or 10 tens as 1 hundred when a place makes 10 or more.\n"
        "- Out of scope: adding with a nearby round number, numbers of more than 3 digits, subtraction.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A petrol station forecourt (soft photo background) with a big red pump whose screen is a chart of three equal columns headed \"hundreds\", \"tens\", \"ones\". Two rows hold 47 and 36 as fuel tokens: 4 amber bars and 7 blue drops, then 3 bars and 6 drops. A carry row above the chart and an answer row below it are empty. Digit tiles 0 to 9 sit under the chart.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Add the two fuel amounts. Start with the ones.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap the ones column.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Tokens: a drop stands for 1 (a thumb-nail wide), a bar is 10 drops joined, a tank is 10 bars joined. Each digit is also printed in large plain type at the left of its token group.\n"
        "- Each column has a carry circle above it and one answer box below it. The columns are wide, so ones sit exactly under ones.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Find the total\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a column: the tokens of both numbers in that column slide down and join, and a counter reads \"13 ones\".\n"
        "- When the counter is 10 or more, tap the glowing 10 tokens: they merge into one token of the next kind, which flies to the carry circle of the next column on the left.\n"
        "- Drag (or tap) a digit tile into the glowing answer box. Columns go in order: ones, tens, hundreds. Each digit is checked when it lands; a wrong digit slides back.\n"
        "- Tapping a carried token sends it back to its column (undo). Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\", \"Next\" (\"Next\" unlocks when the answer row is full).\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Add 47 and 36.\" Goal chip: \"Find the total\". Setup: 47 and 36 as above. Guess: before tapping, the child taps how many ones there are altogether: \"12\", \"13\" or \"14\". Result: the ones join into 13 drops, 10 merge into a bar that flies to the tens carry circle, 3 is written; tens 4 + 3 + 1 = 8; answer 83. Why line: \"7 + 6 = 13: write 3 and regroup 10 ones as 1 ten. 4 + 3 + 1 = 8. So 47 + 36 = 83.\" Help: guided, each column shows its own count and the arrows between columns.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Add 213 and 124.\" Goal chip: \"Find the total\". Guess: \"Does any place make 10 or more?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: 3 + 4 = 7, 1 + 2 = 3, 2 + 1 = 3, nothing to regroup; 337. Why line: \"No place makes 10, so nothing is regrouped: 213 + 124 = 337.\" Help: guided, the counters show 7, 3 and 3.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided, fewer labels). Goal line: \"Add 64 and 95.\" Goal chip: \"Find the total\". Guess: the child taps the place that needs a regroup: \"ones\", \"tens\" or \"none\" (right: tens). Result: 4 + 5 = 9, 6 + 9 = 15, write 5 and regroup 10 tens as 1 hundred; 159. Why line: \"Ones: 4 + 5 = 9. Tens: 6 + 9 = 15, write 5 and regroup 10 tens as 1 hundred. So 64 + 95 = 159.\" Help: counters shown, no arrows.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Add 285 and 167.\" Goal chip: \"Find the total\". Guess: \"Is the sum more than 400 or less than 400?\" (right: more). Result: 5 + 7 = 12, write 2, regroup; 8 + 6 + 1 = 15, write 5, regroup; 2 + 1 + 1 = 4; 452. Why line: \"12 ones: write 2, regroup. 15 tens: write 5, regroup. 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. So 285 + 167 = 452.\" Help: independent, no counters until the child taps.\n"
        "- Activity 5 (independent). Goal line: \"Add 506 and 195.\" Goal chip: \"Find the total\". Guess: the child taps the hundreds digit of the sum: \"6\", \"7\" or \"8\" (right: 7). Result: 6 + 5 = 11, write 1, regroup; 0 + 9 + 1 = 10, write 0, regroup 10 tens as 1 hundred; 5 + 1 + 1 = 7; 701. Why line: \"11 ones: write 1, regroup. 10 tens: write 0, regroup. 5 + 1 + 1 = 7. So 506 + 195 = 701.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"A lorry has 28 litres of fuel in its tank. An additional 75 litres is filled. What is the total quantity of fuel in the lorry?\" Goal chip: \"Total litres\". Guess: \"Will the lorry have more than 100 litres?\" (\"Yes\"/\"No\", right: Yes). Result: 8 + 5 = 13, write 3, regroup; 2 + 7 + 1 = 10, write 0, regroup 10 tens as 1 hundred; 103 litres. Why line: \"28 + 75 = 103: the tank holds 103 litres.\" Help: independent, no counters or arrows.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"Let us try one more. Find the sum of 49 and 89.\" Goal chip: \"Find the sum\". Guess: \"Will the sum have 2 digits or 3 digits?\" (right: 3). Result: 9 + 9 = 18, write 8, regroup; 4 + 8 + 1 = 13, write 3, regroup 10 tens as 1 hundred; 138. Why line: \"Ones: 18, write 8. Tens: 4 + 8 + 1 = 13, write 3. So 49 + 89 = 138.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child sees tokens pile up in a column and, at 10, change into one token of the next column, so a place can never hold more than 9. Adding place by place, starting with the ones, finishes with the regrouped tokens added in.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Add one place at a time, starting with the ones.\" \"10 of a place make 1 of the next place on the left.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the digit lands with a soft click, its box turns green; at the end the whole sum rolls up on the pump screen, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: tries to write 13 in the ones box -> red strip, small shake: \"One place holds one digit. 13 ones is 1 ten and 3 ones: write 3 and regroup the ten.\" The ten tokens glow.\n"
        "- Mistake: leaves out the carried ten (writes 7 for 4 + 3 + 1) -> \"A ten was regrouped. Did you add it? Look at the carry circle.\" The carried bar pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: leaves a box empty or writes 10 when a place makes exactly 10 -> \"10 tens is 1 hundred and 0 tens: an empty place is not a zero, use the 0 tile.\" The box pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: taps tens before ones -> \"Start with the ones: they are the smallest.\" The ones column pulses. Taps regroup on fewer than 10 tokens -> \"There are only 7 ones: nothing to regroup.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Which place do you add first? Look at the ones.\" (2) visual clue: the tokens of that column glow and the counter shows the count. (3) almost the answer: the tool joins the tokens, merges the 10 and writes \"13 ones: write 3 and regroup 1 ten\"; the child only places the digit.\n"
        "- Edge cases: a number with 0 in a place (506 has no tens tokens, the tens row is empty and the carried bar is the only tens token on the left); a place that makes exactly 10 (506 + 195, 28 + 75); a hundreds column that starts empty and only gets the regrouped tank (64 + 95, 49 + 89); a sum with no regrouping (213 + 124).\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Both star challenges are the lorry and the 49 + 89 sums above; success is every digit in place and the whole sum shown.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Add one place at a time, starting with the ones.\" \"10 of a place make 1 of the next place on the left.\" Think and talk: \"What is the biggest sum you can make by adding two 2-digit numbers, and why?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: petrol station forecourt (photo, soft blur); red pump front (photo-style cut-out with a flat screen area); fuel tokens drawn in code (blue drops, amber bars, green tanks); digit tiles drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Near-Round Number Adder\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, Fuel Arithmetic, \"Let Us Solve\" (add the pairs, finding easier ways wherever possible): 15 + 79, 46 + 99, 38 + 35, 5 + 89, 76 + 28, 69 + 20 (developer only). Practice values: 37 + 49, 58 + 198, 83 + 27, 66 + 40, 24 + 68.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student adds a number that is just below a round ten or hundred by adding the round number and taking the extra back, and adds a whole number of tens by adding the tens.\n"
        "- Out of scope: column addition with regrouping, subtracting numbers, numbers above 300.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A carpenter's workbench (photo, soft blur) with two wooden number planks laid end to end on a ruler line marked every 10: a plank of 37 and a plank of 49, each with its number burned into the wood. Under them three empty plates in a row: \"Add the round number\", \"Take the extra back\", \"Total\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Add 37 and 49 the easier way. Tap the number that is close to a round number.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap the 49 plank.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Planks are drawn to scale (1 unit about 4 screen pixels up to 200) with tick marks every 10 and a bolder tick every 100. A dotted outline piece appears at the end of a plank when it is stretched to the next round number; a tag on it names its size, for example \"extra 1\".\n"
        "- A round-number tile (for example \"50\") sits at the end of the stretched plank. For a whole number of tens the plank splits into ten-blocks, each block marked \"10\".\n"
        "- Three plates under the planks: \"Add the round number\", \"Take the extra back\", \"Total\". Answer tiles (three choices) appear on the active plate. Digit tiles 0 to 9 appear in the independent activities.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"37 + 49\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a plank: it stretches to the next round number and the dotted extra piece shows its size. Tap a whole-tens plank: it splits into ten-blocks.\n"
        "- On each plate the child taps one of three answer tiles (the right one and two typical wrong ones). In independent activities and star challenges the child instead taps digit tiles to write the final total.\n"
        "- Dragging the dotted piece off the plank is the \"take back\" move; it slides into the \"Take the extra back\" plate.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (clears the planks and plates of this sum), \"Next\" (\"Next\" unlocks when the total is right). In the star challenges the planks are optional help; any way that gives the right total is accepted.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Add 37 and 49 the easier way.\" Goal chip: \"37 + 49\". Guess: \"If you add 50 instead of 49, did you add too many or too few?\" (\"too many\" is right). Result: the 49 plank stretches to 50 with \"extra 1\"; the child picks 37 + 50 = 87, then takes the extra back: 87 − 1 = 86. Why line: \"49 is 1 less than 50, so 37 + 50 = 87 added 1 too many. 87 − 1 = 86.\" Help: guided, the three plates are labelled.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Add 58 and 198 the easier way.\" Goal chip: \"58 + 198\". Guess: \"How many too many do you add if you add 200 instead of 198?\" chips \"1\", \"2\", \"20\" (right: 2). Result: 58 + 200 = 258, then 258 − 2 = 256. Why line: \"198 is 2 less than 200, so 58 + 200 = 258 added 2 too many. 258 − 2 = 256.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided, labels fade). Goal line: \"Add 83 and 27 the easier way.\" Goal chip: \"83 + 27\". Guess: \"How far below 30 is 27?\" chips \"2\", \"3\", \"7\" (right: 3). Result: 83 + 30 = 113, then 113 − 3 = 110. Why line: \"27 is 3 less than 30, so 83 + 30 = 113 added 3 too many. 113 − 3 = 110.\" Help: the plate labels are hidden until the child taps a plate.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Add 66 and 40 by adding the tens.\" Goal chip: \"66 + 40\". Guess: \"6 tens and 4 tens make how many tens?\" chips \"8\", \"10\", \"24\" (right: 10). Result: the 40 plank splits into 4 ten-blocks that join the 6 tens of 66; 10 tens make 100, the 6 ones stay: 106. Why line: \"40 is 4 tens: 6 tens + 4 tens = 10 tens, which is 100, and the 6 ones stay: 100 + 6 = 106.\" Help: independent, digit tiles only.\n"
        "- Activity 5 (independent). Goal line: \"Add 24 and 68. Choose which number to round.\" Goal chip: \"24 + 68\". Guess: \"Which number is nearer to a round number, 24 or 68?\" chips \"24\", \"68\" (right: 68). Result: 68 is 2 less than 70: 24 + 70 = 94, then 94 − 2 = 92. Rounding 24 up to 30 also works: 30 + 68 = 98, then 98 − 6 = 92; the tool says \"Both ways give 92. The nearer round number means a smaller take back.\" Why line: \"68 is 2 less than 70: 24 + 70 = 94, then 94 − 2 = 92.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"Add the following numbers. Wherever possible, find easier ways to add the pairs of numbers. (a): 15 + 79; (b): 46 + 99; (c): 38 + 35.\" Goal chip: \"Add (a) to (c)\". Guess before each part: the child taps the number they would round, or \"none\". Results: (a): 79 is 1 less than 80, so 15 + 80 = 95 and 95 − 1 = 94. (b): 99 is 1 less than 100, so 46 + 100 = 146 and 146 − 1 = 145. (c): 73; either way is accepted: 38 is 2 less than 40, so 40 + 35 = 75 and 75 − 2 = 73, or by place value 8 + 5 = 13, then 3 + 3 + 1 = 7. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"Add the following numbers. Wherever possible, find easier ways to add the pairs of numbers. (d): 5 + 89; (e): 76 + 28; (f): 69 + 20.\" Goal chip: \"Add (d) to (f)\". Guess: as in Star Challenge 1. Results: (d): 89 is 1 less than 90, so 5 + 90 = 95 and 95 − 1 = 94. (e): 28 is 2 less than 30, so 76 + 30 = 106 and 106 − 2 = 104. (f): 20 is 2 tens: 6 tens + 2 tens = 8 tens, which is 80, and the 9 ones stay: 80 + 9 = 89. Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child sees a plank that is just short of a round length: adding the round length adds a small extra piece too many, and that extra piece must come off again. The size of the extra is the gap to the round number (1, 2 or 3).\n"
        "- A whole number of tens needs no take back: tens add to tens and the ones stay.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Just below a round number? Add the round number, then take the extra back.\" \"A whole number of tens: add the tens.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the dotted piece slides out, the total plate turns green and the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce; the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: forgets to take back (writes 95 for 15 + 79) -> red strip, small shake: \"You added 80, but the number was 79. Take the extra back.\" The dotted piece pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: takes back the wrong amount (258 − 20, or 258 − 1) -> \"How far below 200 is 198? Look at the dotted piece: it is 2.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: adds the extra instead of taking it away (96 for 15 + 79) -> \"The extra piece was added by mistake: it has to come off, not go on.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: taps a number far from a round number (the 83 in Activity 3) -> \"83 is 7 below 90: a long way. Try the other number.\" The other plank pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: forgets the ones in a tens sum (100 for 66 + 40) -> \"The 6 ones of 66 stay. Put them back with the 100.\" The 66 plank's ones pulse.\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Look at the two numbers. Is one just below a round number?\" (2) visual clue: the dotted piece glows and shows its size. (3) almost the answer: the plates fill with \"37 + 50 = 87\" and \"take back 1\", and the child picks the last tile.\n"
        "- Edge cases: Activity 5 has two good choices; the tool accepts either and shows both totals. Star part (c) has no number very close to a round number; place value or rounding is accepted. A sum that crosses a hundred, such as 83 + 27 = 110, is shown with its plank passing the bold 100 tick.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 and 2 are the book's six pairs; success is the right total for every part, whichever way the child found it.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Just below a round number? Add the round number, then take the extra back.\" \"A whole number of tens is added by adding the tens.\" Think and talk: \"When is it easier to use a round number, and when is it easier to add place by place?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: carpenter's workbench (photo, soft blur); wooden plank texture strips (cut-outs) for the number planks and for the dotted extra piece; ruler line with ticks drawn in code; round-number tiles and answer tiles drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M1.S9.T9.C24.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Fuel Gauge Subtractor\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S9.T9.C24.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, \"Relationship Between Addition and Subtraction\" (two question sets), \"More Fuel Arithmetic\" (the minibus, 65 − 18, check 18 + 47 = 65) and \"Let Us Solve\" (82 − 37 and eight subtractions) (developer only). Practice values: 26 + 54 = 80, 83 − 46, 60 − 27.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student subtracts with place value, regrouping 1 ten as 10 ones when there are too few ones, and uses the link between a whole and its two parts to write addition and subtraction sentences and to check an answer by adding.\n"
        "- Out of scope: calculating with more than 2 digits (the large sentence cards only rearrange numbers), other word problems.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A minibus dashboard (photo, soft blur) with a large round fuel gauge dial at the right. On the stage a part-whole bar: a long bar labelled 80 above two shorter bars side by side, 26 and 54. Under it the sentences \"26 + 54 = 80\", \"80 − 54 = ___\" and \"80 − 26 = ___\".\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Look at 26 + 54 = 80. Which number is the whole? Tap it.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap one of the three numbers.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Sentence layout: the part-whole bar, sentence slots, and number tiles with the signs + − = under the sentences. Chart layout: a two-column chart \"tens\" and \"ones\" (the bigger number on top, the smaller below, each digit as amber bars and blue drops), a regroup row above, an answer row below with digit tiles 0 to 9, and the round gauge with two marks (smaller reading, bigger reading) and the difference shaded between them.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Fill both blanks\" or \"83 − 46\". After an answer a small round chip \"Check by adding\" appears on the chart.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Sentence layout: drag number tiles into blanks, or number and sign tiles into an empty sentence row. A wrong tile slides back.\n"
        "- Chart layout: tap the top tens bar to regroup (it breaks into 10 drops that join the ones; the tens go down by 1). Tap a column to take the bottom tokens away from the top; drag a digit tile into the glowing answer box (ones, then tens). Tap \"Check by adding\": a strip fills in, for example \"46 + 37 = 83\", and the needle moves to the bigger reading.\n"
        "- Tap a placed tile to take it back. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (restores this question), \"Next\" (unlocks when the answer is right).\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Fill the blanks: 80 − 54 = ___ and 80 − 26 = ___.\" Goal chip: \"Fill both blanks\". Guess: the child taps the whole among 26, 54 and 80 (right: 80); it gets a gold ring. Then the child drops 26 and 54 into the blanks. Result: 80 − 54 = 26 and 80 − 26 = 54; the bar breaks into its parts. Why line: \"26 + 54 = 80, so 80 − 54 = 26 and 80 − 26 = 54: the whole take away one part is the other part.\" Help: guided, the bar stays on screen.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Subtract 46 from 83.\" Goal chip: \"83 − 46\". Guess: \"Can you take 6 ones from 3 ones?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: 1 ten breaks into 10 drops, giving 13 ones and 7 tens; 13 − 6 = 7 and 7 − 4 = 3; the answer is 37; \"Check by adding\" shows 46 + 37 = 83. Why line: \"3 ones are fewer than 6, so 1 ten becomes 10 ones: 13 − 6 = 7 and 7 − 4 = 3. So 83 − 46 = 37.\" Help: guided, the counts of ones and tens are written under the chart.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Subtract 27 from 60, then check by adding.\" Goal chip: \"60 − 27\". Guess: \"Do you need to regroup?\" (\"Yes\"/\"No\", right: Yes). Result: 0 ones cannot lose 7, so 1 ten becomes 10 ones: 10 − 7 = 3 and 5 − 2 = 3; the answer is 33; \"Check by adding\" must be tapped here: 27 + 33 = 60. Why line: \"0 ones are fewer than 7, so regroup 1 ten: 10 − 7 = 3 and 5 − 2 = 3. So 60 − 27 = 33, and 27 + 33 = 60.\" Help: independent, no counts shown.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"Find the relationship between the numbers in the given statements and fill in the blanks appropriately. (a): If 46 + 21 = 67, then, 67 − 21 = ___. 67 − 46 = ___. (b): If 198 − 98 = 100, then, 100 + ___ = 198. 198 − ___ = 98. (c): If 189 + 98 = 287, then, 287 − 98 = ___. 287 − 189 = ___. (d): If 872 − 672 = 200, then, 200 + ___ = 872. 872 − ___ = 672.\" Goal chip: \"Fill all blanks\". Guess before each part: tap the whole. Results: (a): 46 and 21; (b): 98 and 100; (c): 189 and 98; (d): 672 and 200. Why line, for example (b): \"198 is the whole: 100 + 98 = 198 and 198 − 100 = 98.\" Help: independent, no bar.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"In each of the following, write the subtraction and addition sentences that follow from the given sentence. (a): If 78 + 164 = 242, then, (b): If 462 + 839 = 1,301, then, (c): If 921 − 137 = 784, then, (d): If 824 − 234 = 590, then,\". Goal chip: \"Write sentences\". Guess before each part: tap the whole. The child builds sentences from the three given numbers and the signs; any true sentence of the other kind is accepted. Sample answers: (a): 242 − 164 = 78; (b): 1,301 − 839 = 462; (c): 784 + 137 = 921; (d): 590 + 234 = 824. The strip says \"Your answer may differ.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: \"A minibus has 18 litres of fuel left. After refuelling, the fuel meter indicates 65 litres. How much fuel has been filled in the fuel tank of the minibus?\" Goal chip: \"Fuel filled\". Guess: \"Is the fuel filled more than 40 litres?\" (\"Yes\"/\"No\", right: Yes). Result: 5 ones are fewer than 8, so regroup: 15 − 8 = 7 and 5 − 1 = 4; 47 litres; \"Check by adding\" shows 18 + 47 = 65 and the needle moves to 65. Why line: \"65 − 18 = 47: 47 litres were filled, and 18 + 47 = 65.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line: \"What is the difference between 82 and 37? Check your answer. Is 37 + ___ = 82?\" Goal chip: \"Difference\". Guess: \"Will you need to regroup?\" (\"Yes\"/\"No\", right: Yes). Result: 2 ones are fewer than 7, so regroup: 12 − 7 = 5 and 7 − 3 = 4; the answer is 45 and the blank is 45. Why line: \"82 − 37 = 45 and 37 + 45 = 82.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5. Goal line: \"(a): 57 − 11 = ___; (b): 23 − 19 = ___; (c): 49 − 21 = ___; (d): 56 − 18 = ___; (e): 93 − 35 = ___; (f): 84 − 23 = ___; (g): 70 − 43 = ___; (h): 65 − 47 = ___.\" Goal chip: \"Eight differences\". Guess before each part: \"Regroup?\" with \"Yes\"/\"No\" (Yes for b, d, e, g, h). Results: (a): 46; (b): 4; (c): 28; (d): 38; (e): 58; (f): 61; (g): 27; (h): 18. Why line, for example (h): \"5 ones are fewer than 7: 15 − 7 = 8 and 5 − 4 = 1. So 65 − 47 = 18.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Three numbers are one whole and two parts: the whole take away a part is the other part, and the two parts make the whole. When the ones on top are too few, a ten breaks into ten ones and nothing is lost. The answer added to the smaller number gives the bigger number back, so adding checks subtracting.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"The whole take away one part is the other part.\" \"Not enough ones? Regroup 1 ten as 10 ones.\" \"Check a subtraction by adding the answer to the smaller number.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the digit lands with a soft click and its box turns green; the gauge arc shades to the answer; the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: takes the smaller digit from the bigger (writes 6 − 3 = 3 in the ones) -> red strip, small shake: \"The top number is the one you start with. 3 ones cannot lose 6 ones: regroup 1 ten as 10 ones.\" The top ones pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: forgets to lower the tens after regrouping (8 − 4 = 4) -> \"You broke 1 ten for the ones, so the tens are 7 now, not 8.\" The tens count pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a part used as the whole, or a number outside the three given -> \"Use the same three numbers. The whole is the biggest number.\" The tile slides back and the bar rings the whole.\n"
        "- Mistake: \"Check by adding\" does not match -> \"The check does not match: 46 + 38 is not 83. Look at the ones again.\" The needle stops short.\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Compare the top ones with the bottom ones. Are there enough?\" or \"Which number is the biggest? It is the whole.\" (2) visual clue: the two ones counts glow with a \">\" or \"<\", or the bar rings the whole. (3) almost the answer: the tool regroups and shows \"13 ones take away 6 ones\", or fills one blank.\n"
        "- Edge cases: 0 ones (60, 70), where regrouping gives exactly 10 ones; a tens answer of 0 (23 − 19: the tens box shows 0 and the answer reads 4; \"04\" is accepted); no regrouping needed (57 − 11, 49 − 21, 84 − 23); the big numbers in the sentence cards are only rearranged.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are the book's own questions; success is the right answer in every blank, sentence or part. Challenges 3 and 4 also need \"Check by adding\".\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"The whole take away one part is the other part.\" \"Not enough ones? Regroup 1 ten as 10 ones.\" \"Check a subtraction by adding the answer to the smaller number.\" Think and talk: \"If 90 − 35 = 55, which two addition sentences can you write with these numbers?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: minibus dashboard (photo, soft blur); gauge dial and needle (code); ten-bars, ones-drops, part-whole bar, number, sign and digit tiles (code). All text English."
    ),
    "M2.S13.T13.C36.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Consecutive Sums Lab\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S13.T13.C36.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, Sums of Consecutive Numbers (the three boxes of sums of 2, 3 and 4 consecutive numbers and the three questions about them) (developer only). Practice values: runs of 3 numbers from 20 and 40, of 5 and 6 numbers from 30, of 2 and 4 numbers from 60.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student finds that successive sums of consecutive numbers go up by the number of numbers added, and sees which runs give odd sums, even sums, or sums that take turns.\n"
        "- Out of scope: the pair-the-ends shortcut, runs of more than 6 numbers, proving the parity rule for every length.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A river bank (photo, soft blur) with a row of 12 flat stepping stones, numbered 20 to 31 in white paint. A glowing wooden frame (the window) lights 3 stones: 20, 21 and 22. Under the row a running-total strip, and under it a sum table with the headings \"start\", \"sum\" and \"odd or even\", all empty.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Three stones are lit. Tap them one by one to add them up.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap the stone 20.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Stones: grey cut-outs, each about one twelfth of the stage width, numbers in large plain digits. The window frame has a handle on its right edge; dragging the handle lights 2 to 6 stones. The frame slides along the row one stone at a time.\n"
        "- Running-total strip: shows each addition as it happens, for example \"20\", \"20 + 21 = 41\", \"41 + 22 = 63\". Sum table: up to 5 lines, each with the start number, the sum in large type and an \"odd\" or \"even\" tag; between two lines a small tag shows the difference, for example \"+3\".\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Find the jump\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap the lit stones from left to right: the running total grows. A stone tapped out of order bounces back. When all lit stones are added, the sum becomes a new table line with its tag.\n"
        "- Drag the frame one stone to the right (or tap the stone just right of it) to light the next run. Each activity sets its own row of stones, so the start numbers are always on screen.\n"
        "- Answer chips and reason cards appear for the guess and the questions. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (frame back to its first place, table cleared), \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Add the lit stones, then slide the window.\" Goal chip: \"Find the jump\". Setup: stones 20 to 31, 3 lit. Guess: before the first slide, \"How much will the sum go up when the window moves one stone?\" with chips \"1\", \"3\" and \"20\" (right: 3). Result: 20 + 21 + 22 = 63, then the sums 66, 69 and 72 as the child slides three times; each gap shows \"+3\". Why line: \"21 + 22 + 23 = 66 is 3 more than 63: each of the 3 numbers went up by 1, so the sum went up by 3.\" Help: guided, a small \"+1\" floats over each lit stone when the window slides.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided less). Goal line: \"Light 5 stones, then 6 stones. How much does the sum go up?\" Goal chip: \"Jump for 5 and 6\". Setup: stones 30 to 41, 5 lit. Guess: \"With 5 stones lit, how much will the sum go up?\" with chips \"1\", \"5\" and \"6\" (right: 5). Result: 5 stones: 160 then 165; 6 stones: 195 then 201. Why line: \"165 − 160 = 5 and 201 − 195 = 6: the sum goes up by the number of lit stones.\" Help: no \"+1\" bubbles; the table shows the differences.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Light 2 stones, then 4 stones. Are the sums odd or even?\" Goal chip: \"Odd or even\". Setup: stones 60 to 71. Guess, asked for 2 stones and again for 4 stones: \"all odd\", \"all even\" or \"odd, then even\". Result: 2 stones give 121, 123 and 125, all odd; 4 stones give 246, 250 and 254, all even. Why line: \"60 + 61 = 121, 61 + 62 = 123 and 62 + 63 = 125 are all odd; 60 + 61 + 62 + 63 = 246, then 250 and 254, are all even.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Light 3 stones and slide the window three times. Do the tags take turns?\" Goal chip: \"Odd, even, odd, even\". Setup: stones 40 to 51. Guess: \"Will the tags take turns?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: Yes). Result: the sums are 123 (odd), 126 (even), 129 (odd), 132 (even). Why line: \"40 + 41 + 42 = 123, and each sum is 3 more than the one before. 3 is odd, so odd and even take turns.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"In each of the boxes above, state whether the sums are even or odd. Explain why this is happening.\" Goal chip: \"Even or odd, and why\". Setup: the child rebuilds the three boxes on stones 1 to 8: 2 stones give 3, 5, 7, 9; 3 stones give 6, 9, 12, 15; 4 stones give 10, 14, 18, 22. Guess before each box: \"all odd\", \"all even\" or \"odd and even in turn\". Then the child drags the right reason card (one right, two wrong). Results: Box 1, all odd: \"Two numbers in a row are one even and one odd, and even plus odd is odd.\" Box 2, in turn: \"The sum is 3 times the middle number, so it is even when the middle number is even and odd when it is odd.\" Box 3, all even: \"Four numbers in a row make two pairs of neighbours; each pair adds to an odd number, and odd plus odd is even.\" The tool adds \"Your words may differ.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"What is the difference between two successive sums in each box? Is it the same throughout?\" Goal chip: \"Find the differences\". Guess before each box: chips for three possible differences. Result: Box 1: 2, Box 2: 3, Box 3: 4, the same throughout each box. Why line: \"5 − 3 = 2, 9 − 6 = 3 and 14 − 10 = 4: the difference is the number of numbers added.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: \"What will be the difference between two successive sums for (a): 5 consecutive numbers; (b): 6 consecutive numbers?\" Goal chip: \"5 and 6 stones\". Guess: the child taps the difference for (a), then for (b), before building. Then the child checks with the window on stones 1 to 8. Result: (a): 5; (b): 6. Why line: \"15 and 20 differ by 5, and 21 and 27 differ by 6.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Sliding the window moves every lit number up by 1, so the sum goes up by as many as there are lit stones. The tags show a pattern: 2 lit stones always give odd sums, 4 lit stones always give even sums, and 3 or 5 lit stones give sums that take turns.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"The sum goes up by the number of numbers added.\" \"Two numbers in a row add to an odd number; four numbers in a row add to an even number.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the tag and the difference tag appear in the table, the chosen chip turns green with a gentle bounce, and the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: says the sum goes up by 1 -> red strip, small shake: \"The sum goes up by more than 1. Look at the table: 66 is 3 more than 63.\" (real numbers from the table). The table difference tags pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: wrong tag or chip for odd and even -> \"Look at one pair first: 20 + 21 is even plus odd, and that is odd.\" The first two lit stones pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: stops adding early -> \"Did you add all 3 stones? The total shows 41 so far.\" The unadded stone pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a reason card that does not fit -> \"That does not fit the stones. Look at the tags in the table.\" The card slides back.\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"How many stones are lit? Look at the frame.\" (2) visual clue: the difference tags in the table glow, or a \"+1\" floats over each lit stone as the window slides. (3) almost the answer: the tool slides the window once and writes both sums with the difference between them, for example \"63\" and \"66\" with \"+3\".\n"
        "- Edge cases: the frame stops at 2 and 6 stones and at the ends of the row. A run of 6 stones gives sums that are all odd (21, 27 and 33 from stone 1), and the tool shows the tags without making a rule of it.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 3 are the book's own three questions about the boxes. Success is every box tagged and explained, the three differences found, and 5 and 6 answered.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"The sum goes up by the number of numbers you add.\" \"Two numbers in a row add to an odd number, and four numbers in a row add to an even number.\" Think and talk: \"What is the difference between two successive sums of 10 consecutive numbers, and how do you know?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: river bank (photo, soft blur); flat grey stepping-stone cut-outs, 12 in a row, with numbers painted by code; glowing wooden frame (cut-out); running-total strip, sum table and tags drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M2.S15.T15.C44.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Pair-the-Ends Lab\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S15.T15.C44.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, the patterns in sums of consecutive numbers and the four sums to find without adding directly: 67 + 68 + 69, 24 + 25 + 26 + 27, 48 + 49 + 50 + 51 + 52, 237 + 238 + 239 + 240 + 241 + 242 (developer only). Practice values: runs from 90 (3 numbers), 83 (4), 70 (5) and 15 (6).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student finds the sum of a run of 3 to 6 consecutive numbers by pairing the numbers from the two ends, since every pair makes the same total.\n"
        "- Out of scope: the successive-sum patterns of the Consecutive Sums Lab, runs of more than 6 numbers, any rule written with letters.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A wooden toy-block shelf (photo, soft blur). Three wooden number blocks 90, 91 and 92 stand in a row on the shelf, each block about a hand-width wide with its number in large plain digits. Above the row, an empty ribbon arch; under the shelf a sum box, locked, and two read-outs \"pairs\" and \"pair total\", empty.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Pair the ends of the row. Find the sum of 90, 91 and 92.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap the first block, 90.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Blocks: 3 to 6 per row, evenly spaced. When two blocks are paired, a curved ribbon arch joins them and a brass tag hangs under the arch with the pair total, for example \"90 + 92 = 182\".\n"
        "- A block with no partner stays in place with a small label \"no partner\".\n"
        "- Sum box: an empty box with digit tiles 0 to 9 under the shelf; it stays locked until every pair is made. Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Sum of 3\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap the first block and then the last block (or drag one onto the other): both hop under a ribbon arch and the tag shows their total. Then tap the next two blocks from the outside in. Tapping two blocks that are not the outermost pair that is left bounces them back.\n"
        "- When no block can be paired any more, the sum box unlocks; the child taps digit tiles to write the sum. In the star challenge the blocks can only be paired, never added one by one.\n"
        "- Tap a ribbon to unpair its blocks. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (clears all pairs), \"Next\" (\"Next\" unlocks when the sum is right).\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Pair the ends. Find the sum of 90, 91 and 92.\" Goal chip: \"Sum of 3\". Guess: \"Which block has no partner?\" The child taps one block (right: 91). Result: the arch joins 90 and 92 with 90 + 92 = 182; 91 stands alone; the sum is 182 + 91 = 273. Why line: \"90 + 92 = 182 and the 91 stands alone: 182 + 91 = 273.\" Help: guided, a dotted arch shows where the first pair goes.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Pair the ends. Find the sum of 83, 84, 85 and 86.\" Goal chip: \"Sum of 4\". Guess: \"Will both pairs make the same total?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: Yes). Result: 83 + 86 = 169 and 84 + 85 = 169; no block is left alone; the sum is 169 + 169 = 338. Why line: \"Both pairs make 169, so the sum is 169 + 169 = 338, which is 2 × 169.\" Help: no dotted arch.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"A friend says the sum of 70, 71, 72, 73 and 74 is 5 × 70 = 350. Pair the ends to check.\" Goal chip: \"Is 350 right?\" Guess: \"Is the friend right?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: 70 + 74 = 144, 71 + 73 = 144, the 72 stands alone: 144 + 144 + 72 = 360. Why line: \"The sum is 360, which is 5 × 72. The friend used the first number: 5 × 70 = 350 is 10 too small.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Pair the ends. Find the sum of 15, 16, 17, 18, 19 and 20.\" Goal chip: \"Sum of 6\". Guess: \"How many pairs will there be?\" with chips \"2\", \"3\" and \"6\" (right: 3). Result: 15 + 20 = 35, 16 + 19 = 35 and 17 + 18 = 35; the sum is 35 + 35 + 35 = 105. Why line: \"3 pairs of 35 make 105.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"Notice how the sums of 3, 4, and 5 consecutive numbers are related to the numbers being added. Use your understanding to find the following sums without adding the numbers directly. (a): 67 + 68 + 69; (b): 24 + 25 + 26 + 27; (c): 48 + 49 + 50 + 51 + 52; (d): 237 + 238 + 239 + 240 + 241 + 242.\" Goal chip: \"Four sums\". Guess before each part: \"Is a block left without a partner?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (yes for a and c, no for b and d). Results: (a): 67 + 69 = 136 and the 68 stands alone: 136 + 68 = 204. (b): 24 + 27 = 51 and 25 + 26 = 51: 51 + 51 = 102. (c): 48 + 52 = 100 and 49 + 51 = 100, the 50 stands alone: 100 + 100 + 50 = 250. (d): 237 + 242 = 479, 238 + 241 = 479 and 239 + 240 = 479: 479 + 479 + 479 = 1,437. Help: independent, no dotted arch.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Pairing from the outside in, the child sees that every pair makes the same total: moving inwards, the left block of each pair goes up by 1 and the right block comes down by 1. With an odd number of blocks the middle block stands alone and is half of every pair total (72 is half of 144).\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Pair the ends: every pair makes the same total.\" \"The sum is the pairs added up, plus the middle number if there is one.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the sum box turns green, the sum rolls up, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows; the strip adds the other way to say it, for example \"5 × 72 = 360\".\n"
        "- Mistake: writes the count times the first number (350 for 70 to 74) -> red strip, small shake: \"The first block is the smallest, so 5 × 70 is too small. Look at the middle block.\" The 72 pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: leaves out the block with no partner (writes 288 for 70 to 74) -> \"One block had no partner. Did you add the 72?\" The lone block pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: pairs the first two blocks -> \"Pair the two ends first: the first block and the last block.\" The two end blocks pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: tries to add the numbers one by one in the star challenge -> \"Try pairing the ends instead of adding one by one.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Look at the first and the last block. What do they make together?\" (2) visual clue: the two end blocks glow and a ribbon with their total appears. (3) almost the answer: the tool pairs the first two blocks and writes the pair totals; the child adds them and the middle block.\n"
        "- Edge cases: an odd number of blocks (3 or 5) has one block with no partner; an even number (4 or 6) has none. Six blocks make 3 pairs. A run with 3-digit numbers (237 to 242) works the same way.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is the book's own question; success is all four sums right, each found by pairing.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Pair the first and the last number: every pair makes the same total.\" \"A middle number with no partner stands alone.\" \"The sum is the pair totals added up, plus the middle number if there is one.\" Think and talk: \"Why is the middle number half of every pair total?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: wooden toy-block shelf (photo, soft blur); wooden number blocks (cut-out block face, numbers drawn by code); ribbon arch and brass tag (cut-outs); digit tiles drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M2.S17.T17.C48.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Line Up and Solve\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S17.T17.C48.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, adding and subtracting large numbers, with the journeys, routes and the Let Us Solve and Let Us Do sets (developer only). Practice values: 481 + 56, 7,258 + 14,963, 8,305 − 2,467, 9,640 − 640.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student adds and subtracts numbers of up to 5 digits by lining the digits up from the right and working one place at a time, regrouping where a place has too many (adding) or too few (subtracting).\n"
        "- Out of scope: place names beyond ten thousands, answers of more than 6 digits, multiplying.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A toll plaza (photo, soft blur) with a large dark board of five equal columns; a dot marks the right edge of the ones column and no place names show. Number strips 481 and 56 wait at the left with a \"+\" tile. A regroup row above the board, an empty answer row below, digit tiles 0 to 9 under it.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Line up 481 and 56. Put each strip on the board with its last digit on the dot.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to drag the strip 481.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Board rows: regroup marks, two numbers with the sign tile, a thick line, answer. A sixth column opens on the left only when an answer needs it and is never named. Strips are dark plates, one white digit per cell. A small \"names\" icon shows place names for 3 seconds.\n"
        "- Road trip: a code-drawn route with five city dots and labelled legs. Closest sums: five number tiles and a target ribbon. Goal chip, for example \"481 + 56\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Drag a strip onto the board: it snaps with its last digit in the ones column; elsewhere it slides back. In the stars, numbers of one length arrive lined up; a shorter number arrives at the left and must be dragged into place.\n"
        "- Work from the right. Adding: when a column makes 10 or more, tap it to put a small 1 above the next place. Subtracting: tap a column to regroup (the left place goes down by 1, this place up by 10). Then drag a digit tile into the glowing answer box. Closest sums: tap two tiles to bring them over.\n"
        "- Tap a placed digit or mark to take it back. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\", \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Line up the numbers, then add.\" Goal chip: \"481 + 56\". Guess: \"Which digit of 481 will the 5 of 56 sit under?\" chips \"4\", \"8\", \"1\" (right: 8). Result: 6 under 1 and 5 under 8; 1 + 6 = 7, 8 + 5 = 13, write 3 and regroup, 4 + 1 = 5; the sum is 537. Why line: \"8 + 5 = 13: write 3 and regroup 1. So 481 + 56 = 537.\" Help: guided, a thin guide runs down the columns.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Line up the numbers, then add.\" Goal chip: \"7,258 + 14,963\". Guess: \"How many digits will the sum have?\" chips \"4\", \"5\", \"6\" (right: 5). Result: the first four places make 11, 12, 12 and 12, so a 1 is regrouped each time; the sum is 22,221. Why line: \"Each place makes 11 or 12, so a 1 is regrouped each time: 7,258 + 14,963 = 22,221.\" Help: guided, less.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided). Goal line: \"Subtract. The numbers are already lined up.\" Goal chip: \"8,305 − 2,467\". Guess: \"Can 5 ones lose 7 ones?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: no ten to regroup, so 1 hundred becomes 10 tens, then 1 ten becomes 10 ones; 15 − 7 = 8, 9 − 6 = 3, then 12 − 4 = 8 and 7 − 2 = 5 after regrouping 1 thousand; the difference is 5,838. Why line: \"No ten, so go to the hundreds first: 8,305 − 2,467 = 5,838.\" Help: guided, the changed numbers show above each column.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Line up the numbers, then subtract.\" Goal chip: \"9,640 − 640\". Guess: \"Will you need to regroup?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: 640 sits under the hundreds, tens and ones; 6 − 6, 4 − 4 and 0 − 0 leave 0; the 9 has nothing under it and comes down: 9,000. Why line: \"Nothing is under the 9, so it comes down: 9,640 − 640 = 9,000.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star guess (every item): the child taps the first digit of the answer from three chips (the right one and two others). Each regroup then shows one working line, such as \"Tens: 5 + 6 = 11, write 1 and regroup\". Help in all stars: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"One of the places on the North-South Corridor was 1,855 km from Srinagar and 1,862 km from Kanniyakumari. What was the total length of the North-South Corridor in 2019?\" then \"Mahesh and his family decide to drive from Srinagar to Kanniyakumari. He spends ₹21,880 on fuel and toll tax, and ₹38,900 on other expenses during this journey. How much did he spend in total?\" Goal chip: \"Two totals\". Results: 1,855 + 1,862 = 3,717 km; 21,880 + 38,900 = 60,780, so ₹60,780.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"Find the following sums. Try not to write ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens and ones at the top. Just align the digits properly, at least for the smaller numbers. (a): 238 + 367; (b): 1,234 + 12,345; (c): 12 + 123; (d): 46,120 + 12,890; (e): 878 + 8,789; (f): 1,749 + 17,490.\" Goal chip: \"Six sums\". Results: (a): 605; (b): 13,579; (c): 135; (d): 59,010; (e): 9,667; (f): 19,239.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: \"The great Indian road trip! Nazrana and her friends planned a road trip across India, starting from Delhi. They first drove to Mumbai, then Goa, then Hyderabad, and finally Puri. Look at the distances marked on the map and help them find the total distance travelled.\" then \"Find 2 numbers among 5,205, 6,220, 7,095, 8,455, and 4,840 whose sum is closest to the following. (a): 10,000; (b): 15,000; (c): 13,000; (d): 16,000.\" Goal chip: \"Trip and closest\". Results: 1,600 + 590 + 670 + 1,055 = 3,915 km. Closest pairs: (a): 5,205 and 4,840 (10,045); (b): 6,220 and 8,455 (14,675); (c): 8,455 and 4,840 (13,295); (d): 7,095 and 8,455 (15,550). For each pair the strip shows how far the sum is from the target.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line: \"The total distance of the sea route from Mumbai to Chennai is 2,700 km. A ship starting from Mumbai first reaches the Cochin port, travelling 1,083 km by sea. How much more distance does it have to travel to reach the Chennai port?\" then \"As you learnt earlier, the longest land route is 15,150 km between Talon (Russia) and Sagres (Portugal). The longest highway in Africa is 10,228 km long, connecting the cities of Cairo, in Egypt and Cape Town, in South Africa. How much longer is the land route between Talon and Sagres compared to the highway between Cairo and Cape Town?\" Goal chip: \"Two differences\". Results: 2,700 − 1,083 = 1,617 km; 15,150 − 10,228 = 4,922 km.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5. Goal line: \"Subtract the following. Try not to write ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens and ones at the top. Align the digits carefully. (a): 4,578 − 2,222; (b): 15,324 − 11,780; (c): 5,423 − 423; (d): 123 − 12; (e): 77,777 − 777; (f): 826 − 752.\" Goal chip: \"Six differences\". Results: (a): 2,356; (b): 3,544; (c): 5,000; (d): 111; (e): 77,000; (f): 74.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 6. Goal line: \"Add. (a): 2,009 + 7,388; (b): 26,444 + 71,111; (c): 777 + 888; (d): 1,234 + 1,234; (e): 56 + 56,789; (f): 777 + 77,777; (g): 5,922 + 9,221; (h): 4,321 + 8,765; (i): 50,050 + 55,000.\" then \"Subtract. (a): 458 − 226; (b): 7,777 − 4,449; (c): 65,447 − 47,299; (d): 1,234 − 123; (e): 12,345 − 1,234; (f): 56,789 − 56; (g): 87,326 − 11,111; (h): 878 − 52; (i): 749 − 222.\" Goal chip: \"Eighteen answers\". Results, adding: (a): 9,397; (b): 97,555; (c): 1,665; (d): 2,468; (e): 56,845; (f): 78,554; (g): 15,143; (h): 13,086; (i): 1,05,050. Subtracting: (a): 232; (b): 3,328; (c): 18,148; (d): 1,111; (e): 11,111; (f): 56,733; (g): 76,215; (h): 826; (i): 527.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Lining up puts like places in one column. When a column cannot hold its answer, 10 of a place become 1 of the next place on the left (adding), or 1 becomes 10 of the place on the right (subtracting); a 0 place is skipped by going one place further left. A digit with nothing under it comes down.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Line up the digits from the right.\" \"Work one place at a time, starting with the ones.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: each digit lands with a soft click and turns green, the answer shows in large type, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce.\n"
        "- Mistake: a strip in the wrong column (56 left-aligned) -> red strip, small shake: \"The last digit of every number sits in the ones column. The 6 goes under the 1.\" The dot pulses; the strip slides back.\n"
        "- Mistake: writes 13 in one box or forgets the regrouped 1 -> \"One place holds one digit: write 3 and regroup the 1.\" The marks pulse. Takes the smaller digit from the bigger -> \"5 cannot lose 7: regroup first.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: subtracts across a 0 -> \"There is no ten to regroup, so go to the hundreds first.\" Forgets the lowered place -> \"You regrouped 1 hundred, so the hundreds are 2 now, not 3.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a place makes 10 in 50,050 + 55,000 -> \"10 in this place: write 0 and put the 1 in a new place on the left.\" The sixth column opens. A pair that is not the closest -> \"A closer pair exists. Try another pair.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Where is the ones digit of each number? Look at the right end.\" (2) visual clue: guide lines run through the columns and the working column glows. (3) almost the answer: the tool places the shorter strip and works the first column.\n"
        "- Edge cases: numbers of different lengths (12 + 123); a 0 in the middle (8,305); zeros in answers (5,000; 77,000); columns of 0 − 0; the six-digit answer 1,05,050.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 6 are the book's own questions; success is every answer right.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Line up the digits from the right, ones under ones.\" \"Work one place at a time, starting with the ones.\" \"Regroup when a place has too many or too few.\" Think and talk: \"Why must the last digit of both numbers sit in the same column?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: toll plaza (photo, soft blur); board, strips, tiles and route map drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M3.S25.T25.C84.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Round Number Hops\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M3.S25.T25.C84.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, \"Quick Sums and Differences\": Sukanta's blanks, the units digit 0, Namita's take away 9 or 99 and the blanks that leave 9 or 99 (developer only). Practice values: 63, 425, 6,284, 84 − 9, 342 − 99.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student finds what to add to reach 100, 1,000 or 10,000 by making 9 in every place and adding 1, and takes away 9 or 99 by taking away 10 or 100 and giving 1 back.\n"
        "- Out of scope: other round numbers such as 500, subtracting numbers other than 9 and 99.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A long straight road (photo, soft blur) with a signpost \"100\" at the far end. A code-drawn number line runs along it from 63 to 100 with ticks every 10; a toy car waits on 63. Above the digits 6 and 3 two empty boxes wait for their nine partners. Digit tiles 0 to 9 sit under the road.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"How much do you add to 63 to reach 100? Make a 9 with each digit.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap the digit 6.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Number line: re-drawn for each question, from the start number to the signpost (100, 1,000 or 10,000), with tick marks. The car hops along it; each hop is drawn as an arc with its size written on it.\n"
        "- Make panel (pills that add): a box above each digit of the start number for its partner, so that digit and partner make 9. Take-away panel (pills that subtract): the start number, and two hop tiles \"Hop back 10\" and \"Hop back 100\" and one tile \"Hop forward 1\".\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"63 + ___ = 100\". The blank is a box that takes digit tiles.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Make pills: tap a digit, then tap the digit tile that makes 9 with it; the partner box fills. When all partners are in, the car hops to the 99 mark (99, 999 or 9,999) by the number they make. Tap the small signpost \"Add 1\": the car hops 1 to the round number and the blank number grows by 1. Then tap digit tiles to write the blank.\n"
        "- Take-away pills: tap \"Hop back 10\" or \"Hop back 100\", then \"Hop forward 1\"; the number after each hop is written on the line. Then tap digit tiles to write the answer.\n"
        "- Tap the last hop on the line to undo it. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (the car goes back to the start), \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Fill the blank: 63 + ___ = 100.\" Goal chip: \"63 + ___ = 100\". Guess: \"Is the blank less than 40 or more than 40?\" (right: less). Result: partners 3 and 6; the car hops 36 to 99, then 1 more to 100; the blank is 37. Why line: \"6 and 3 make 9, and 3 and 6 make 9: 63 + 36 = 99. 99 is 1 less than 100, so 63 + 37 = 100.\" Help: guided, lines join each digit to its partner.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Fill the blanks: 425 + ___ = 1,000 and 6,284 + ___ = 10,000.\" Goal chip: \"Two blanks\". Guess before each blank: \"Is the blank more than 500 or less?\" for 425 (right: more) and \"more than 5,000 or less?\" for 6,284 (right: less). Results: 425 + 574 = 999, so the blank is 575; 6,284 + 3,715 = 9,999, so the blank is 3,716. Why line: \"The partners of 425 are 5, 7 and 4: 425 + 574 = 999, and 1 more makes 425 + 575 = 1,000.\" Help: partner lines appear only when the child taps a digit.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided). Goal line: \"Take away 9 and 99: 84 − 9 and 342 − 99.\" Goal chip: \"Hop back, hop forward\". Guess: \"Taking away 10 instead of 9 takes away too many or too few?\" (right: too many). Results: 84 − 10 = 74, then 74 + 1 = 75; 342 − 100 = 242, then 242 + 1 = 243. Why line: \"10 is 1 more than 9, so give 1 back: 84 − 9 = 75. 100 is 1 more than 99, so 342 − 99 = 243.\" Help: guided, the \"Hop forward 1\" tile pulses after each hop back.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"Sukanta likes the numbers 10, 100, 1,000, and 10,000. He wants to figure out what number he should add to a given number such that the sum is 100 or 1,000. Help him fill in the blank with an appropriate number. 32 + ___ = 100\" then \"Try this method for the number 59. 59 + ___ = 100\". Goal chip: \"Two blanks\". Guess before each blank: \"Is the blank more or less than half of the round number?\" (50 for 100). Results: 32 + 68 = 100 (partners 6 and 7 make 67, add 1); 59 + 41 = 100 (partners 4 and 0 make 40, add 1). Help: independent, no partner lines.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"Now, use this method to solve the following. 877 + ___ = 1,000 and 666 + ___ = 1,000; 4,103 + ___ = 10,000 and 5,555 + ___ = 10,000.\" Goal chip: \"Four blanks\". Guess before each blank: more or less than half of the round number (500 or 5,000). Results: 877 + 123 = 1,000; 666 + 334 = 1,000; 4,103 + 5,897 = 10,000; 5,555 + 4,445 = 10,000. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: \"Will this method work if the units digit is 0? What do you think? What other methods can you use to find the missing number to fill in the blanks? Share your thoughts in the class. (a): 180 + ___ = 1,000; (b): 760 + ___ = 1,000; (c): 400 + ___ = 1,000.\" Goal chip: \"Does it work with 0?\" Guess: \"Will the method work with a 0?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: Yes). Results: (a): 820; (b): 240; (c): 600. The child picks a reason card: \"The last nine is 9, and adding 1 makes it carry.\" Another way: 180 + 20 = 200, then 200 + 800 = 1,000. The strip says \"Your words may differ.\"\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line: \"Namita likes the number 9. She wants to subtract 9 or 99 from any number. Find a way to quickly subtract 9 or 99 from any number. (a): 67 − 9 = ___; (b): 83 − 9 = ___; (c): 144 − 9 = ___; (d): 187 − 99 = ___; (e): 247 − 99 = ___; (f): 763 − 99 = ___.\" Goal chip: \"Six differences\". Guess before each: \"Which hop back will you use, 10 or 100?\" Results: (a): 58; (b): 74; (c): 135; (d): 88; (e): 148; (f): 664. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5. Goal line: \"Now, use the above solutions to find answers to the following problems. Do not calculate again. Namita wonders if she can get 9 or 99 as the answer to any subtraction problem. Find a way to get the desired answer. (a): 32 − ___ = 9; (b): 56 − ___ = 9; (c): 877 − ___ = 99; (d): 666 − ___ = 99.\" Goal chip: \"Four blanks\". Guess before each: \"32 − 9 or 32 + 9?\" (right: take 9 away). Results: (a): 23; (b): 47; (c): 778; (d): 567. Why line: \"9 and the blank are the two parts of 32, so the blank is 32 − 9 = 23, and 32 − 23 = 9.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Making 9 in every place always leaves exactly 1 to reach the round number, because 99, 999 and 9,999 are each 1 less than 100, 1,000 and 10,000. A 0 digit needs a 9, so numbers ending in 0 work too. Taking away 10 takes 1 too many from a 9, and 100 takes 1 too many from a 99, so 1 is given back. A blank that leaves 9 is the first number minus 9.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Make 9 in every place, then add 1.\" \"Take away 10 or 100, then give 1 back.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the car lands on the signpost, the blank turns green, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: stops at 99 (writes 36 for 63 + ___ = 100) -> red strip, small shake: \"63 and 36 make 99, not 100. 99 is 1 less than 100: add the 1.\" The \"Add 1\" signpost pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a partner that makes 10 (4 for the 6) -> \"6 and 4 make 10. Which digit makes 9 with 6?\" The box pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a 0 digit given a partner of 0 -> \"A 0 needs a 9 to make 9: 0 + 9 = 9.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: stops after \"Hop back 10\" -> \"You took away 10. Did you give 1 back? 10 is 1 more than 9.\" \"Hop forward 1\" pulses. Wrong hop size (100 for 9) -> \"100 is far too many for 9. Hop back 10 for 9 and 100 for 99.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: adds 9 where a blank leaves 9 (41 for 32 − ___ = 9) -> \"9 and the blank are the two parts of 32. The blank is 32 − 9.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"What does each digit need to make 9?\" or \"9 is 1 less than 10: what do you do after hopping back 10?\" (2) visual clue: the partner boxes light up, or the car hops back and shows where it lands. (3) almost the answer: \"63: the partners make 36, so 63 + 36 = 99. Add the extra 1.\" or \"84 − 9: hop back 10 to 74, then forward 1 to 75.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: a 0 digit (180, 760, 400, and the 0 in 4,103) has the partner 9; a 9 digit (59) has the partner 0; repeated digits (666, 5,555); a number ending in 0 carries when 1 is added, as in 819 + 1 = 820.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are the book's own questions; success is every blank right.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Make 9 in every place, then add 1 to reach 100, 1,000 or 10,000.\" \"To take away 9 or 99, take away 10 or 100, then give 1 back.\" Think and talk: \"Why does making 9 in every place and then adding 1 always reach a round number?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: long straight road (photo, soft blur); roadside signposts for 100, 1,000 and 10,000 (cut-outs); a toy car (flat drawing, front view and side view); number line, partner boxes, hop arcs and tiles drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M3.S30.T30.C106.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Number Grid Puzzle\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M3.S30.T30.C106.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Puzzle\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, \"Let Us Think and Solve\", the 3×3 number grids with inc and dec arrows: all inc, all dec, and three mixed grids (developer only). Practice values: a 2×2 grid with 1 to 4, a repair grid 1, 2, 3 over 4, 6, 5 over 7, 8, 9, and rows inc with columns dec.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student fills a number grid with 1 to 9 so that every row and column follows its inc or dec arrow, by first finding where the smallest and the biggest number must go.\n"
        "- Out of scope: counting how many grids exist, grids larger than 3×3, any numbers other than 1 to 9.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- The tiled floor of a railway waiting hall (photo, soft blur). On the stage a 2×2 grid of four large square floor tiles. Right of each row a brass plate \"dec\" with a small arrow → ; under each column a plate \"dec\" with a small arrow ↓. Under the grid a tray holds four brass number plates 1, 2, 3 and 4.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Fill the grid with 1 to 4. Every row and column must be dec. Which tile holds the 4?\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap a tile.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Grid tiles are about 90 pixels square on a tablet and the grid is the largest thing on screen. Plates are about a thumb-width, one of each number. Rows are read left to right and columns top to bottom.\n"
        "- An arrow plate turns green when its line is full and follows its arrow, and glows orange when its line is full and breaks the arrow.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"All dec\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- First tap the tile for the smallest or biggest number (the guess). Then drag plates from the tray into tiles; drag a plate back to the tray or onto another plate in the grid to swap them. A plate is never refused: a line that breaks its arrow just glows orange.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (all plates back to the tray), \"Next\" (\"Next\" unlocks when every arrow plate is green).\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Fill the grid so every row and column is dec.\" Goal chip: \"All dec\". Guess: \"Tap the tile where the 4 must go.\" (right: top left). Result: for example 4, 3 over 2, 1 (4, 2 over 3, 1 also works). Why line: \"Every row and column shrinks, so the biggest number starts both its row and its column: 4 goes top left and 1 goes bottom right.\" Help: guided, a small arrow on each tile shows which way its line shrinks.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Fix the grid: swap two plates so every row and column is inc.\" Goal chip: \"Fix it\". Setup: 1, 2, 3 over 4, 6, 5 over 7, 8, 9, all arrows inc; the middle row glows orange. Guess: \"Tap the row or column that breaks its arrow.\" (right: the middle row). Result: swap 6 and 5, so the middle row is 4, 5, 6. Why line: \"The middle row read 4, 6, 5 and 6 is more than 5. After the swap every row and column grows.\" Help: guided, the orange glow marks the line.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Fill the grid: every row inc, every column dec.\" Goal chip: \"Rows inc, columns dec\". Setup: 3×3 grid, plates 1 to 9, arrows inc on all rows and dec on all columns. Guess: \"Tap the tile for 9.\" then \"Tap the tile for 1.\" (right: top right, then bottom left). Result: for example 7, 8, 9 over 4, 5, 6 over 1, 2, 3 (many grids work). Why line: \"A row grows to the right and a column shrinks downwards, so 9 is at the end of its row and the top of its column: top right. 1 is bottom left.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"In a 3×3 grid, arrange the numbers 1 to 9 such that each row and each column has numbers in an increasing (inc) order. Each number should be used only once.\" Goal chip: \"All inc\". Guess: the tiles for 1 and 9 (top left, bottom right). Result: for example 1, 2, 3 over 4, 5, 6 over 7, 8, 9. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"This time, fill the grid such that each row and column has numbers in decreasing (dec) order.\" Goal chip: \"All dec\". Guess: the tiles for 9 and 1 (top left, bottom right). Result: for example 9, 8, 7 over 6, 5, 4 over 3, 2, 1. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: \"Now, fill the grids below with numbers (1 to 9) based on the inc (increasing) and dec (decreasing) conditions, as indicated below. Grid 1: the rows are dec, dec, dec and the columns are inc, inc, inc.\" Goal chip: \"Grid 1\". Guess: the tiles for 1 and 9 (top right, bottom left). Result: for example 3, 2, 1 over 6, 5, 4 over 9, 8, 7. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line: \"Fill the grid with numbers 1 to 9 as indicated. Grid 2: the rows are dec, dec, inc and the columns are dec, dec, inc.\" Goal chip: \"Grid 2\". Guess: the tiles for 1 and 9; two tiles can hold each (1 at top right or bottom left, 9 at top left or bottom right) and either is accepted. Result: for example 8, 6, 1 over 7, 5, 2 over 3, 4, 9. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 5. Goal line: \"Fill the grid with numbers 1 to 9 as indicated. Grid 3: the rows are inc, inc, dec and the columns are inc, dec, dec.\" Goal chip: \"Grid 3\". Guess: the tiles for 1 and 9; 1 at top left or bottom right, 9 at top right or bottom left. Result: for example 1, 6, 8 over 2, 5, 7 over 9, 4, 3. Help: independent.\n"
        "- In every star the tool says \"Your grids may differ\" and accepts any grid that follows every arrow.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child finds that the smallest number must be the first tile of every growing line and the last tile of every shrinking line it sits in, and the biggest number the opposite, so the arrows tell the corners. Once 1 and 9 are placed the other plates are filled and every row and every column is checked, not only the rows.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Rows are read left to right and columns top to bottom.\" \"The arrows tell where the smallest and the biggest number go.\" \"Check every row and every column.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: every arrow plate turns green, the plates make a soft click, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the grid is read out as three rows.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong tile for the smallest or biggest -> red strip, small shake: \"The smallest number is the first of a growing line or the last of a shrinking line, in its row and in its column. Look at the arrows.\" The two arrow plates of that tile pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: a row breaks its arrow -> \"Row 2 should grow to the right, but 6 comes before 5. Swap them.\" (real numbers.) A column breaks -> \"Rows are fine, but column 3 should shrink downwards and 5 comes before 9.\" The orange plate pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: reads a column from the bottom -> \"Columns are read from top to bottom.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Which arrow plates are orange? Look at the rows and the columns.\" (2) visual clue: the tiles that can hold 1 and 9 are outlined. (3) almost the answer: the tool places 1 and 9 in a working pair of tiles and you fill the other plates.\n"
        "- Edge cases: many grids are right (42 for the first three grids and 60 for Grids 2 and 3), so the tool says \"This grid works too.\" In Grids 2 and 3 two tiles can hold 1 and two can hold 9. All dec in a 2×2 grid has two right grids.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 5 are the book's own five grids; success is every arrow plate green for each grid.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Rows are read left to right and columns top to bottom.\" \"The arrows tell where the smallest and the biggest number go.\" \"Check every row and every column.\" Think and talk: \"In a grid where every row and column is inc, why must 9 be in the bottom right corner?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: railway waiting hall floor (photo, soft blur); large square floor tile cut-out (for the grid cells); brass number plates and brass arrow plates (cut-outs, numbers and words drawn by code). All text English."
    ),
    "M4.S34.T34.C126.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Pairs and Leftovers\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M4.S34.T34.C126.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, \"Even and Odd Numbers\", the paired arrangement of counters and the question about the sums of pairs of even numbers, of odd numbers, and of an odd and an even number (12 and 6, 13 and 9, 7 and 12) (developer only). Practice values: 16, 21 and 30 counters; 24 + 32, 11 + 15, 25 + 4.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student uses the leftover counters to explain that even plus even is even, odd plus odd is even and odd plus even is odd.\n"
        "- Out of scope: adding three or more numbers, subtracting, multiplication facts about even and odd.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A wooden board on a bench at a bus stand (photo, soft blur). A heap of 16 glossy blue round counters, drawn as shaded discs, lies in a drop zone on the board. Two answer cards, \"even\" and \"odd\", sit under the zone.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Is 16 even or odd? Guess, then press Pair up.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap a card.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Counters: shaded blue discs, each about a thumb-nail wide (they shrink to fit up to 60). In pairs they lie in two rows, so each top counter sits above its partner. A counter with no partner gets a small orange ring.\n"
        "- Pairing activities show one drop zone; combining activities show a Group 1 zone, a Group 2 zone and a combine zone between them. Beside a group sits its number in large type and, after the answer, an \"even\" or \"odd\" tag.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Even or odd\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap \"even\" or \"odd\" first (the guess). The main button reads \"Pair up\": it arranges a group in two rows and rings a leftover. In the combining activities the main button then reads \"Combine\": the two groups slide together, the rings move side by side and the joined group is paired again. Then \"Next\".\n"
        "- In the star challenges two number wheels show the numbers the question allows (even numbers from 2 to 40, odd numbers from 1 to 39); the child turns them to choose a pair and presses \"Add\". Five pairs fill a list of five lines.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (puts this card's counters back) and the main button.\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Is each number even or odd? Guess, then pair up.\" Goal chip: \"Even or odd\". Setup: 16, then 21, then 30 counters. Guess: \"even\" or \"odd\" before pairing each. Results: 16 makes 8 pairs, none left, even; 21 makes 10 pairs and 1 is left, odd; 30 makes 15 pairs, none left, even. Why lines: \"16 counters make 8 pairs with none left over, so 16 is even.\" \"21 counters make 10 pairs and 1 is left over, so 21 is odd.\" Help: guided, the pairs are counted aloud.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Join two groups. Will a counter be left over?\" Goal chip: \"Even and even\". Setup: Group 1 has 24 counters, Group 2 has 32. Guess: \"After joining, will a counter be left over?\" with \"Yes\" and \"No\" (right: No). Result: each group pairs with none left, so the joined 56 counters make 28 pairs; 24 + 32 = 56 is even. Why line: \"Neither group has a counter left over, so the joined group has none: 24 + 32 = 56 is even.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Join two groups. Will a counter be left over?\" Goal chip: \"Odd and odd\". Setup: 11 and 15 counters. Guess: as in Activity 2 (right: No). Result: each group has 1 ringed counter; when joined the two rings meet and make a pair, so 26 counters make 13 pairs. Why line: \"11 and 15 each have 1 left over. The two leftovers make a pair, so 11 + 15 = 26 is even.\" Help: the rings pulse before Combine.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"Join two groups. Will a counter be left over?\" Goal chip: \"Odd and even\". Setup: 25 and 4 counters. Guess: as above (right: Yes). Result: 25 has 1 leftover and 4 has none, so 1 stays alone: 14 pairs and 1 left, 29. Why line: \"Only 25 has a counter left over, and it stays alone, so 25 + 4 = 29 is odd.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line, with the opening sentences shared by all three stars: \"What do you notice about the sums in each of the following cases? Do you think it will be true for all pairs of such numbers? Explain your observations. You may use the paired arrangement to explain your thinking.\" then \"(a): 12 and 6 are a pair of even numbers. Choose 5 such pairs of even numbers. Add the numbers in each of the pairs.\" Goal chip: \"5 pairs of even numbers\". Guess: before the first pair, \"What will the sums be?\" with \"all even\", \"all odd\" or \"some of each\". The wheels start on 12 and 6. After 5 pairs the child taps what they noticed, taps \"Yes\" or \"No\" for \"true for all pairs?\", and picks the reason card. Sample answer: 12 + 6 = 18, 4 + 8 = 12, 10 + 20 = 30, 14 + 16 = 30, 2 + 6 = 8; all even, for all pairs. Reason: \"Two even numbers pair up with none left over, so together they pair up with none left over.\" The strip says \"Your pairs may differ.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: the opening sentences of Star Challenge 1 followed by \"(b): 13 and 9 are a pair of odd numbers. Choose 5 such pairs of odd numbers. Add the numbers in each of the pairs.\" Goal chip: \"5 pairs of odd numbers\". Wheels start on 13 and 9. Sample answer: 13 + 9 = 22, 3 + 5 = 8, 7 + 11 = 18, 15 + 1 = 16, 9 + 9 = 18; all even, for all pairs. Reason: \"Each odd number has 1 left over, and the two leftovers make a pair.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line: the opening sentences of Star Challenge 1 followed by \"(c): 7 and 12 are a pair of odd and even numbers. Choose 5 such pairs of odd and even numbers. Add the numbers in each of the pairs.\" Goal chip: \"5 pairs, odd and even\". The first wheel shows odd numbers and the second even numbers, starting on 7 and 12. Sample answer: 7 + 12 = 19, 3 + 4 = 7, 5 + 10 = 15, 9 + 2 = 11, 11 + 8 = 19; all odd, for all pairs. Reason: \"The odd number has 1 left over and the even number has none, so 1 is still left over.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- The child sees a leftover counter ringed in each group and what happens to the rings on joining: none and none stay none; two rings meet and make a pair; one ring stays alone. So an even number means pairs with none left over, and an odd number means one left over.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Even plus even is even.\" \"Odd plus odd is even: the two leftovers make a pair.\" \"Odd plus even is odd: one leftover stays alone.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the answer card turns green, the joined group settles into its rows, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong tag (21 called even) -> red strip, small shake: \"Look for a ringed counter. Is one left over?\" The ringed counter pulses. For 30: \"Pair them up: 15 pairs and none left over, so 30 is even.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: says odd plus odd is odd -> \"Two leftovers make a pair. Join the two ringed counters.\" Both rings pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: says even plus even leaves a counter -> \"Neither group has a ringed counter, so nothing is left in the joined group.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a repeated pair in a star challenge -> \"You chose that pair already. Pick a new pair.\" Fewer than 5 pairs -> \"Choose 5 pairs. You have 4 so far.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Are the counters in pairs? Look for a counter with no partner.\" (2) visual clue: the ringed counters glow. (3) almost the answer: the tool pairs and joins the groups and shows the rings, for example \"11 and 15: each has 1 left over. They make 1 pair.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: no leftover; two leftovers that make one pair; 30, which ends in 0 and is even; the same number twice in a pair (9 + 9); the wheels never offer a number of the wrong kind.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 to 3 are the book's own three questions; success is 5 different pairs with their sums, what was noticed, and the reason card chosen.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Even plus even is even.\" \"Odd plus odd is even: the two leftovers make a pair.\" \"Odd plus even is odd: one leftover stays alone.\" Think and talk: \"Why do the two leftover counters of two odd numbers always make a pair?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: bus stand bench (photo, soft blur); wooden board (cut-out); shaded blue counters (3D-style discs, one image reused); small orange ring; number wheels and answer cards drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
    "M4.S40.T40.C144.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Table Explorer\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M4.S40.T40.C144.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: We the Travellers - II, \"Let Us Think\" (Mountain climbing: the table of seven peaks, with Priyanka Mohite's heights and years) and \"Math Metric Mela\" (the table of certificates printed and students attended for three districts) (developer only). Practice values: a train table, a guard born 30 years before 2018, and a bus stand table.\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student reads a table by its row and column, finds the highest and lowest values and the difference between two values, and decides whether a printed number is sufficient, short or extra.\n"
        "- Out of scope: drawing tables, tables with more than 3 columns, comparing more than two values at once.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A station information board in a station hall (photo, soft blur). The board is a large table with the headings \"Train\", \"Distance (km)\" and \"Year started\" and four rows: Train A 2,865 2009; Train B 1,978 2014; Train C 3,412 2011; Train D 2,149 2018. Under it an empty compare strip \"___ − ___ = ___\" and digit tiles 0 to 9.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Which cell shows how far Train C runs? Tap it.\"\n"
        "- The child is invited to tap a cell.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- The table is the largest thing on screen; numbers are in large plain type and every column heading has its unit. The book's own tables are drawn exactly as printed: the seven peaks with Height (in metres) and Climbed in the Year, and the three districts with Certificates Printed and Students Attended.\n"
        "- Compare strip under the board: two number slots with a minus sign and an answer slot; the bigger number always goes first. Answer cards \"Sufficient\", \"Short\", \"Extra\" appear for printed and attended numbers.\n"
        "- Goal chip on the stage, for example \"Train C's distance\".\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Tap a row label and a column heading: the row and the column light up in two colours and the cell where they meet glows orange. Tap two numbers of one column: they drop into the compare strip, bigger first. Tap digit tiles to write the difference, a year or a count.\n"
        "- For printed and attended numbers, tap \"Sufficient\" or \"Short\" first; the answer slot is then named \"extra\" or \"short\".\n"
        "- Tap a number in the compare strip to take it out. Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (clears this card), \"Next\".\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Tap Train C and the Distance column. What is where they meet?\" Goal chip: \"Train C's distance\". Guess: before any row or column is tapped, the child taps the cell they think holds it. Result: the cell glows and the strip reads \"Train C, Distance (km): 3,412\". Why line: \"Train C is the row and Distance is the column: they meet at 3,412 km.\" Help: guided, the row and the column light up as the child taps.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided, less). Goal line: \"Which train runs the longest distance? How much longer is it than the shortest? What is the difference between Train A and Train D?\" Goal chip: \"Compare distances\". Guess: the child taps the number they think is the biggest in the Distance column. Result: Train C, 3,412 km; the shortest is Train B, 1,978 km; 3,412 − 1,978 = 1,434 km; Train A and Train D: 2,865 − 2,149 = 716 km. Why line: \"3,412 is the biggest and 1,978 is the smallest: 3,412 − 1,978 = 1,434 km.\" Help: the compare strip appears when two numbers are tapped.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (guided, less). Goal line: \"A guard was 30 years old when Train D started in 2018. In which year was the guard born?\" Goal chip: \"Guard's birth year\". Guess: \"Was the guard born before 2018 or after 2018?\" (right: before). Result: 2018 − 30 = 1988. Why line: \"The guard was 30 in 2018, so the guard was born 30 years earlier: 2018 − 30 = 1988.\" Help: the strip shows the year and the age.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"For each stand, were the tickets sufficient? How many were short or extra?\" Goal chip: \"Three stands\". Setup: a bus stand table with Tickets Printed and Passengers Came: stand P 14,320 and 14,095; stand Q 9,850 and 10,240; stand R 21,730 and 21,050. Guess before each stand: \"Sufficient\" or \"Short\". Results: P: sufficient, 14,320 − 14,095 = 225 extra; Q: short, 10,240 − 9,850 = 390 short; R: sufficient, 21,730 − 21,050 = 680 extra. Why line: \"14,320 tickets for 14,095 people: more tickets than people, so enough, and 225 are extra.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line: \"Priyanka Mohite is the first Indian woman to climb five Himalayan peaks above 8,000 metres. Read the table and answer the questions that follow. (a): Which is the highest peak she climbed? (b): What is the difference in height between the highest and lowest peaks she has climbed, as per the table? (c): What is the difference between heights of Mount Elbrus and Mount Kanchenjunga? (d): If Priyanka was 20 years old when she summited Mount Everest in 2013, in which year was she born?\" Goal chip: \"Four answers\". Setup: the seven-peak table (the book's dashes in the brackets are written as \"and\"): Mount Kanchenjunga (India and Nepal border) 8,586 metres, 2022; Mount Everest (Nepal and China border) 8,848, 2013; Mount Makalu (China and Nepal border) 8,485, 2019; Mount Lhotse (Tibet and Nepal border) 8,516, 2018; Mount Kilimanjaro (Africa) 5,895, 2016; Mount Elbrus (Russia) 5,642, 2017; Mount Annapurna I (Nepal) 8,091, 2021. Guess: before (a), tap the height you think is the biggest; before (b), \"Is the difference more than 3,000 metres?\"; before (c), \"Is it less than the answer to (b)?\"; before (d), \"Born before or after 2013?\" Results: (a): Mount Everest; (b): 8,848 − 5,642 = 3,206 metres; (c): 8,586 − 5,642 = 2,944 metres; (d): 2013 − 20 = 1993. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line: \"For each district, find out if the number of certificates were sufficient? If insufficient, calculate how many certificates fell short. If extra, calculate how many certificates were in excess.\" Goal chip: \"Three districts\". Setup: Chittoor, A.P. 18,225 printed and 18,104 attended; Jaunpur, U.P. 19,043 and 19,265; Raigad, Maharashtra 20,863 and 19,974. Guess before each district: \"Sufficient\" or \"Short\". Results: Chittoor: sufficient, 18,225 − 18,104 = 121 extra; Jaunpur: not sufficient, 19,265 − 19,043 = 222 short; Raigad: sufficient, 20,863 − 19,974 = 889 extra. Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- A table puts facts in rows and columns, and one row and one column meet at exactly one cell. To compare two values the child puts them side by side and takes the smaller from the bigger. When what was printed is compared with what was needed, the bigger one tells the story: more printed is enough, and the difference is how many extra; fewer printed is not enough, and the difference is how many short.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Read the row and the column, and find where they meet.\" \"To find how much more, take the smaller from the bigger.\" \"Printed fewer than needed: the difference is how many are short.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the answer slot turns green, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce and the why line shows; a check by adding appears, for example \"1,978 + 1,434 = 3,412\".\n"
        "- Mistake: taps a wrong cell, row or column -> red strip, small shake: \"That is the row for Train B. Which row is Train C?\" or \"That is the Year column. The distance is in km: look at the Distance heading.\" The right row or column pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: adds instead of subtracts -> \"To find how much more, take the smaller from the bigger. Do not add.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong difference (for example 1,534 for 3,412 − 1,978) -> \"Check the places again: where a place has too few, regroup 1 from the place on its left.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: says sufficient when printed is less (9,850 tickets for 10,240 people) -> \"9,850 tickets for 10,240 people: fewer tickets than people, so the tickets are short.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: adds the age to the year (2048 for the guard) -> \"The guard was born before 2018, so take the age away from the year.\"\n"
        "- Hint button, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Which row is the thing you want and which column is the fact? Look where they meet.\" (2) visual clue: the row and the column light up and cross. (3) almost the answer: the compare strip fills with the two numbers, for example \"3,412 − 1,978 = ___\", and the child writes the digits.\n"
        "- Edge cases: the highest and lowest of 7 rows; a difference where a place has too few, even across a 0 (10,240 − 9,850); a table whose second column is bigger in some rows and smaller in others (Jaunpur is short while Chittoor and Raigad have extra).\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenges 1 and 2 are the book's own two tables with every part answered.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Read the row and the column, and find where they meet.\" \"To find how much more, take the smaller from the bigger.\" \"Fewer printed than needed means short; more printed means extra.\" Think and talk: \"How can you tell at once, without subtracting, whether the tickets are enough?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: station hall information board (photo, soft blur); a small code-drawn mountain peaks strip above the mountain table (no portrait photo); a stack of certificates (photo cut-out) beside the certificate table; the tables, strip and tiles drawn in code. All text English."
    ),
}

OBJECTIVES = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": "The student sees that swapping one card between two groups raises one sum and lowers the other by the same amount, so the gap between the two sums changes.",
    "M1.S4.T4.C14.2D1": "The student adds two numbers one place at a time, starting with the ones, and regroups 10 of a place as 1 of the next place on the left.",
    "M1.S7.T7.C20.2D1": "The student adds a number just below a round ten or hundred by adding the round number and taking the extra back, and adds whole tens by adding the tens.",
    "M1.S9.T9.C24.2D1": "The student subtracts with place value, regrouping 1 ten as 10 ones when there are too few ones, and uses a whole and its two parts to write sentences and to check by adding.",
    "M2.S13.T13.C36.2D1": "The student finds that successive sums of consecutive numbers go up by the number of numbers added, and sees which runs give odd sums, even sums or sums that take turns.",
    "M2.S15.T15.C44.2D1": "The student finds the sum of a run of consecutive numbers by pairing the numbers from the two ends, because every pair makes the same total.",
    "M2.S17.T17.C48.2D1": "The student adds and subtracts numbers of up to 5 digits by lining the digits up from the right and working one place at a time, regrouping where a place has too many or too few.",
    "M3.S25.T25.C84.2D1": "The student finds what to add to reach 100, 1,000 or 10,000 by making 9 in every place and adding 1, and takes away 9 or 99 by taking away 10 or 100 and giving 1 back.",
    "M3.S30.T30.C106.2D1": "The student fills a 3 by 3 grid with 1 to 9 so that every row and column follows its inc or dec arrow, by first finding where the smallest and the biggest number go.",
    "M4.S34.T34.C126.2D1": "The student uses leftover counters to explain that even plus even is even, odd plus odd is even and odd plus even is odd.",
    "M4.S40.T40.C144.2D1": "The student reads a table by its row and column, finds the highest and lowest values and the difference between two values, and decides whether a printed number is sufficient, short or extra.",
}
